# The contract

The contract is the HQL half of the package: the file a program is checked
against. This is `modules/hello/mod.hql`, whole:

````text
/// Greetings, provided by the `hello` package.
module hello

import core

/// A greeting and the number of characters in it.
type Greeting { text : String, length : Int }

/// The greeting of a name: `Greeting("Ada")` holds `Hello, Ada!`.
/// `punctuation` replaces the `!`.
fn Greeting(name: String, *, punctuation: String = "!") : Greeting

/// The text greeting a name: `greet("Ada")` is `Hello, Ada!`.
fn greet(name: String) : String

/// `self` in capitals: `"hi".shout()` is `HI`.
fn shout(self: String) : String

/// The text of a greeting in capitals.
fn shout(self: Greeting) : String

/// The sum of `weight` over the elements of `self`.
fn total<T>(self: List<T>, weight: T -> Int) : Int

/// A function that panics, which the call reports as a failure.
fn fail(message: String) : String
````

## The head of the file

A contract opens with `module` and the module's name, which is the name
`provides` lists and the path of the file under the module root. A file
whose `module` line names another module is refused.

`import core` makes the types of the language's core visible: `String`,
`Int`, `List`. A contract is a module like any other, a closure over its
own imports ([[05-modules/names|chapter 5]]), so it imports every module
whose types it writes. The prelude does not apply inside a package.

## Types

`type Greeting { text : String, length : Int }` declares a record type.
A type a contract declares belongs to the language once the package is
loaded: a program that imports `hello` reads `g.text`, and the checker
knows it is a `String`.

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

Greeting("Ada")
```

```text
Greeting(text = "Hello, Ada!", length = 11)
```

## A constructor

`fn Greeting(…) : Greeting` is a declaration named as its type is: a
**constructor**. A record type without one is constructed by naming every
field. With one, a call of the type's name reaches the package's function,
which computes the fields: here `length` is counted, never passed.

## Declarations

The remaining lines are declarations
([[32-functions-and-declarations/declarations|chapter 32]]):

- `greet` takes one `String` and has no receiver, so it is called
  `hello.greet("Ada")`.
- `shout` has two forms, on a `String` and on a `Greeting`, both receiver
  calls: `"hi".shout()`.
- `total` is generic over the element type and takes a function,
  `T -> Int`.
- `Greeting` takes `punctuation` by name only, after `*`, with a default.

<!-- run: --workspace examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello

["hi".shout(), Greeting("Ada").shout()]
```

```text
["HI", "HELLO, ADA!"]
```

## Doc strings

A `///` paragraph directly above a declaration is its doc string. It is
the text `hql help` prints, so it is written for the caller: what the
function gives, with one example. Forms of one name written in one
paragraph share a doc string.

## What the contract does not say

The contract states no implementation, and the Rust states no types. A
change to a signature is a change to this file alone, as long as the Rust
function still reads the values the new signature admits. A change to what
a function computes leaves this file untouched.
